/*  stopPreT -- THE PARSE-DEAD GUARANTEE HAS A PRECONDITION, AND THIS IS IT.
    Built 2026-09-17 for fixIts F-78, on Tony's ruling.

    "Everything below stop() is parse-dead and therefore unconstrained" is ratified
    doctrine (Addendum 2, Tony 2026-08-20) and every fixit citizen's explanation prose
    rests on it. ⚠ IT HOLDS ONLY WHILE NO REFUSAL IS OUTSTANDING.

    MEASURED, three instruments agreeing:
      an action call after a refusal DOES NOT RUN     -- a noop spliced into trigDO ran
                                                         when called BEFORE the refusal
                                                         and not when called after
      stopParsingInput IS NEVER ENTERED               -- its own measureStopCaller callout
                                                         fires once in a clean run and
                                                         ZERO times with a refusal standing
      plain statements KEEP RUNNING                   -- SP-1 and SP-2 both print
    ⚠ SO stop() IS NOT DEFECTIVE. Its body carries no refused guard and works perfectly
    when reached. It is never reached: an outstanding refusal silences ACTION AND COMMAND
    DISPATCH, and stop() is a command. More gets parsed, not less.

    THE ROWS
      SP-1  a plain statement after the refusal still runs -- the refusal is NOT a halt
      SP-2  ⚠ THE FINDING: a statement BELOW stop() runs, because stop() never fired
      SP-3  the refusal itself, by message
      SP-4  the abandonment line, by message
    ⚠ SP-2 IS PINNED AS THE WRONG BEHAVIOUR ON PURPOSE (H7's other half). It is not a
    green claim that this is right -- it is a pin so that the day stop() starts stopping,
    something says so. Its header says that in terms.
    ⚠ AND SP-1 IS SP-2'S ANTI-VACUITY SIBLING: without it, SP-2 could pass on a run where
    everything after the refusal ran for some unrelated reason. SP-1 says execution
    continued; SP-2 says it continued PAST A STOP.
    There is no sentinel and no reachable stop(): the ABANDONED line is the terminal
    marker, emitted from main after the parse returns, and a truncation cannot fake it.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(StopPreT);
define
    spRoot;
    ;
spX := spRoot.*spNope;
cerr "SP-1 a plain statement after the refusal STILL RAN":;
stop();
cerr "SP-2 a statement BELOW stop() RAN -- stop() was never entered":;
